---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const t = [
  ['11 Mar 1941', 'Henrique born', 'Ivone is seventeen. The father is a Pinto da Rocha. The son stays behind when she goes south.', ''],
  ['2 Feb 1948', 'Ivone marries de Witt', 'At Germiston. He is 24, born in Johannesburg in December 1923.', 'contradiction'],
  ['19 Jun 1948', 'Ivone marries Kurt Luwinski', 'At Johannesburg. Certificate 858. She is entered as Ivone de Figueiredo de Witt, aged 24. He is 36.', 'key'],
  ['after Jun 1948', 'Kurt files for divorce', 'The tree records it as an attempted divorce filed, citing the South African national archives. Weeks, at most, after the wedding.', 'key'],
  ['c. late Jul 1948', 'Derrick conceived', 'Inferred from the birth, counting back.', 'inferred'],
  ['24 Apr 1949', 'Derrick born', '', 'key'],
  ['1951', 'Divorce granted', 'Illiquid case. Restitution of conjugal rights. Kurt Luwinski versus Ivone, formerly de Witt, born Figueiredo.', 'key'],
  ['13 Feb 1953', 'Ivone marries Morris Salkow', 'Entered as Ivone Luwinski.', ''],
  ['29 Feb 1956', 'Kurt marries Frances Hostowsky', 'Registered in the Hanover, Orange Free State records.', ''],
  ['1957', 'Kurt files for divorce again', 'Versus Frances, formerly Sefton, formerly Berner, born Hostowsky.', ''],
  ['Nov 1962', 'Henrique reaches Johannesburg', 'Twenty-one years after his birth, and fourteen after his mother married Kurt.', ''],
  ['15 Dec 1969', 'Kurt dies at Johannesburg', 'Derrick is twenty. Estate file 7584/1969, Pretoria. Buried Chevra Kadisha.', ''],
];
---
<Base title="1948"
  subtitle="Two marriages, one divorce action, and a child"
  lede="Set the dates in a column and the year stops looking like a marriage and starts looking like something else. None of this was noticed until the DNA gave a reason to look.">

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {t.map(([d, e, n, k]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{d}</strong></td>
          <td>{e}{k === 'contradiction' && <> <span class="tag disp">Contradiction</span></>}{k === 'inferred' && <> <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></>}</td>
          <td class="small muted">{n}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What is odd about it</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The gap.</strong> Four and a half months between marrying de Witt and marrying Luwinski,
    with no divorce recorded in between. That is either bigamy or a wrong date, and the archive's
    reading is that it is a wrong date — because she married Kurt <em>as</em> Mrs de Witt, so the de Witt
    marriage must have come first, and probably well before 2 February 1948.
    <a href={base + '/ivone/'}>The argument in full →</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The filing.</strong> Kurt files for divorce within weeks of his own wedding, and before his
    wife gives birth. That is not a marriage that wore out. Something was wrong with it almost at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The timing of the birth.</strong> Derrick was born ten months after the wedding, so the
    conception falls after it. On its face that is unremarkable. It only becomes remarkable alongside
    a DNA result showing <a href={base + '/dna/'}>no trace of Kurt's father or Kurt's mother</a> in the
    child.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A warning about reading backwards <span class="tag inf">Method</span></h3>
    <p>
      Everything on this page was in the record before the DNA was looked at, and none of it was thought
      to mean anything. That should make anyone careful. A timeline read in the light of a conclusion
      will always seem to support it.
    </p>
    <p>
      So: the dates here are documented and the inference is labelled as an inference, and they are kept
      apart on purpose. If the Y-DNA comes back Ashkenazi, this page stays exactly as it is and the
      conclusion goes — a marriage in trouble from the first month, and nothing more.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What sits behind it</h2>
  <p>
    Kurt was thirty-six, fifteen years out of Germany, and had five years earlier lost his father to a
    transport from Berlin he can only have learned about second-hand and late. Ivone was
    twenty-four, born in Portuguese East Africa, with a seven-year-old son in another country. They
    married in June, he sued in the winter, the child came in the autumn, and the court took until 1951
    to let them go. Neither of them was in the country either had been born in.
  </p>
</Base>
